Now I understand the fuzz harness. The key path is:
1. `mg_mqtt_parse(data, size, 5, &mm)` - parse as MQTT v5
2. If it returns `MQTT_OK`, then iterate `mg_mqtt_next_prop(&mm, &prop, ofs)` 

The vulnerability is in `mg_mqtt_next_prop`:
```c
uint8_t *i = (uint8_t *) msg->dgram.buf + msg->props_start + ofs;
uint8_t *end = (uint8_t *) msg->dgram.buf + msg->dgram.len;
size_t new_pos = ofs, len;
prop->id = i[0];  // <-- READ happens BEFORE bounds check!

if (ofs >= msg->dgram.len || ofs >= msg->props_start + msg->props_size || (i + 1) >= end)
  return 0;
```

The `prop->id = i[0]` reads 1 byte from `i` before checking bounds. If `ofs` is large enough that `i` points past the end of the buffer, this is a heap-buffer-overflow READ of 1 byte.

Let me look at `mg_mqtt_parse` to understand how `props_start`, `props_size`, and `dgram` are set.

